// Built-in card templates for the Templates gallery (card-editor.html/js).
// Plain data, embedded directly in a normal <script> tag rather than
// fetched as JSON -- fetch() is blocked by the browser on a page opened
// straight from disk (file://...), which is a very likely way to end up
// testing this editor, while a <script src> tag works there exactly the
// same as on a real server or GitHub Pages. Each entry's front/back is
// the same Fabric canvas JSON the Save button produces, generated once
// by actually building the design in the editor -- and every shape's
// raw fill/stroke is set to match its assigned cardFinish's real
// proofing color (see FINISH_COLORS), not left at the default White, so
// each template looks correct the instant it loads rather than only
// after someone re-clicks its own finish button.
//
// The first three apply everyday layout principles: readable hierarchy
// in 1-2 seconds (name/title biggest, contact smallest), generous edge
// margins instead of crowding the safe zone, and one deliberate accent
// finish (Metallic or Frosted White) rather than several competing ones.
// The next two lean into premium/luxury card conventions instead -- a
// restrained monogram as the single focal point, and a hexagonal crest
// badge (a knockout-monogram emblem rendered in the Texture finish,
// reading as a single logo mark rather than a decorative pattern)
// standing in for a full-card texture wash. The last two build actual
// repeating geometric patterns out of many small Texture-finish shapes --
// a chevron/mountain-range stripe along one edge, and a diagonal diamond
// lattice filling a whole column -- rather than one single accent shape.
// Every shape in both keeps its bounding box (including the rotated
// diamonds' diagonal extent) fully inside the safe zone, not just its
// center point.
//
// The last three are reference-inspired (real business-card photos the
// user shared): Line Cube is a single isometric-cube mark -- a hexagon
// plus three spokes to alternating corners, all one Stroke-finish Path --
// with no text at all on the front, matching a minimalist icon-forward
// card. Shape Scatter tiles circle/triangle/square outlines (Stroke
// finish) across the whole card in a deterministic jittered grid, with a
// corner text block sitting on top of the pattern rather than beside it.
// Circle Badge pairs a Texture-finish circular monogram with a framed,
// group-centered front (like Bold Statement/Deco Crest) and, on the
// back, a two-column bulleted contact list (small filled circles standing
// in for the reference photo's literal social-media icons, which aren't
// worth approximating badly in plain shapes).
//
// Every template's typography and major layout split follow the golden
// ratio (phi ~= 1.618): each hierarchy step down (name -> title ->
// contact) is sized to roughly 1/phi of the one above it, and wherever a
// template has an asymmetric split (Classic Split's divider, Foil
// Monogram's text column) that split sits at the golden point of the
// safe-zone width (~61.8%/38.2%) instead of dead center. Centered/framed
// designs (Bold Statement, Deco Crest, Circle Badge) keep their symmetry
// -- phi is applied to their type scale and internal vertical rhythm
// instead, since forcing an asymmetric split into a deliberately centered
// composition would fight the design rather than improve it.
//
// Every name/email/phone/address here is a recognized placeholder
// convention, not a real (or real-sounding) identity: "Doe" as the
// surname, example.com (IANA-reserved for documentation, RFC 2606), the
// 555-0100-0199 phone block reserved by NANP for fictional use, and a
// generic "123 Main St" address.
